WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.540
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.460
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.580
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.040
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.220
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.020
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.720
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.120
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.680
Today we discover how plant-based
diets improve the performance

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.451
of athletes and
nonathletes alike.

00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:33.060
In my video about comparing vegetarian
and vegan athletic performance,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.780
endurance, and strength, I discussed
a 2020 study that found

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.900
that vegan athletes—even though
they were significantly older—

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.100
had significantly superior
aerobic capacity and endurance,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.540
lasting 25 percent longer on a
time-to-exhaustion cycling test.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.420
The question is why?

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.480
One potential mechanism
that could explain the greater level

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.420
of endurance performance
in vegans may be a higher amount

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.240
of carbohydrate intake, which could
lead to better endurance

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.000
performance through higher
muscle glycogen storage.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.360
Other potential mechanisms
that may explain the better endurance

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.300
performance in vegans could
be due to the anti-oxidant

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.940
and anti-inflammatory
profiles of their diet.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.240
Maybe it’s even their hearts.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.560
Yet another study showing superior
VO2 max in vegan athletes,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.480
meaning superior
aerobic capacity:

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.060
this time they also
did echocardiograms,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.460
looking at their hearts
in real-time using ultrasound,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.800
and the lower relative
wall thickness

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.560
and better main ventricle
systolic and diastolic function

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.020
in the vegans are most
likely positive ﬁndings.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.280
Now wait a second. Given
the higher VO2 max reached

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.460
by the vegan athletes, maybe
they were just better trained

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.920
than the nonvegan athletes,
and that’s why their hearts

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.240
looked like they
were working better.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.480
However, the weekly training
frequency and running distance

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.720
were similar in both groups,
suggesting benefits

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.880
even with the same
amount of training.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.700
So, it’s important to educate
healthcare professionals;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.920
so they don’t try
to discourage a vegan diet

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.620
and may even want to consider
telling folks implementing

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.960
an exercise training
program to give it a try.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.020
But you don’t know if it
has the same kinds of effects

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.440
in nonathletes, until
you...put it to the test.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.220
A vegetarian vs. conventional
calorie-restricted diet:

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.120
the effect on physical fitness
in response to aerobic exercise

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.160
in patients with
type 2 diabetes.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.160
Diabetics were randomized
to the same caloric restriction,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.400
the same exercise, but just
vegetarian versus nonvegetarian.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.880
They provided all the meals
so they could ensure compliance

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.100
and closely monitored
the exercising.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.360
VO2 max increased by 12 percent
in the vegetarian group,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.480
significantly better than in
the non-vegetarian group

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.520
who didn’t significantly
improve at all.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.080
Maximal performance increased
by 21 percent in the vegetarian group,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.960
again, significantly better than in
the non-vegetarian group

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.900
who didn’t significantly
improve at all.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.320
In other words, the results indicated
that more plant-based diets

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.960
led more effectively to
improvement in physical ﬁtness

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.760
than less plant-based diets,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.199
after the same aerobic
exercise program.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.160
It seems that those eating vegetarian

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.660
were able to better burn off

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.940
carbohydrates compared
to nonvegetarians,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.920
and had better insulin sensitivity,

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.280
both markers of improved
metabolic ﬂexibility,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.440
meaning the ability
to switch back and forth

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.940
between burning sugar and fat.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.520
Besides physiological
mechanisms,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.680
there may also be
psychological factors.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.640
They observed reduced hunger
and reduced feelings of depression

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.100
in the vegetarian group
which may have given them

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.260
a more positive attitude
towards exercise.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.600
Here’s the psychological data.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.960
Those randomized to eat vegetarian
had a greater improvement

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.280
in quality of life and mood.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.000
They felt less constrained,
meaning the calorie restriction

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.560
didn’t seem as burdensome;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.480
they had less disinhibition,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.760
meaning less tendency
to binge and overeat,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.400
along with maybe
less feelings of hunger.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.020
Not to mention the superior effects
of a vegetarian diet

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.200
on body weight, glycemic control,
blood lipids,

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.420
insulin sensitivity,
and oxidative stress.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.180
Wait, better body weight?

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.220
I thought they were given
the same number of calories.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.580
Yes, both diets were isocaloric,
the same calories,

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.200
yet just eating meat-free led
to significantly more weight loss—

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.460
about six pounds more;
more waist loss, a slimmer waist;

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.880
lower cholesterol, of course;
and less superficial fat,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.620
meaning the external jiggly fat;

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.180
and most importantly, significantly
more visceral fat loss,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.360
the most metabolically
dangerous deep belly fat.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.480
Same calories, yet more
loss of body fat.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.440
And not surprisingly,
better control of their diabetes.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.320
All in addition to leading
more effectively

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.903
to improvements
in physical ﬁtness.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.790
Did you know that long-
term plant-based eating

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:50.040
may improve exercise
capacity and endurance?

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.382
Here’s the story.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.220
Few studies have investigated
the impact of a plant-based diet

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.160
on athletic performance, but
the majority of the studies

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:04.020
that have been done show no differences
in endurance, performance, or strength.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.080
So, while plant-based diets
do not seem to provide advantages

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.480
or disadvantages
on exercise performance,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.492
what plant-based diets can do
is reduce the risk of chronic disease.

00:05:13.492 --> 00:05:15.120
This is a point
I made in my video

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.640
Why All Athletes Should
Eat Plant-Based Diets,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.220
because surprisingly,
endurance athletes may have

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.340
more advanced atherosclerosis
and more heart muscle damage,

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.680
compared with sedentary individuals.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.140
So, it’s even more important
they eat healthy.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.540
But due to the favorable impact
on health,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.900
it could be assumed that performance
would also be influenced

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.920
by plant-based diets.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.100
Let’s take a closer look
at the available evidence.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.440
This is the most commonly cited review.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.260
Studies connecting vegetarian diets
to improved health

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:44.520
are well-established;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.580
however, the evidence for this phenomenon
to be transferred

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.700
to improved physical performance
in athletes is less clear,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.100
finding no differences
—at least acutely—

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.980
between a vegetarian-based diet
and an omnivorous diet

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.540
in muscular power, muscular strength,
short burst, or endurance performance.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.820
The intervention studies in this review,
however, only lasted days or weeks.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.360
So, being a vegetarian for four days
may not tip the balance,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.200
or even a few months, but that’s
a considerable limitation.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.720
These are people
who have been eating meat

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:16.440
their whole lives

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.540
and subsequently adopt
a vegetarian diet only

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.840
for the duration of the study,
rather than comparing participants

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.440
who have adhered to a vegetarian
or meat-containing diet long-term.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.060
This study compared exercise capacity
of vegan, vegetarian,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.700
and meat-eating recreational
runners and found

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.460
similar maximum power output
among all three groups,

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.060
suggesting there’s no significant difference
in maximum exercise capacity.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.540
But that’s at the same training frequency,
time, and distance.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.420
Perhaps plant-based diets
might enhance recovery

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.060
and allow such athletes
to train longer and harder?

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.940
A number of studies have come out
since this review

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.940
was published in 2016.
What’s the update?

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.240
Well, this study compared
the cardiorespiratory fitness

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.300
and peak torque strength differences
between vegetarian

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.820
and omnivore endurance athletes.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.400
Most of the vegetarians
were actually vegans

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.040
and most or at least two years and…

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.020
results from this study indicate that

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.780
vegetarian endurance athletes’
cardiorespiratory ﬁtness

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.160
was greater than that for their
omnivorous counterparts.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:18.600
They had a greater VO2 max,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.840
meaning a greater maximal oxygen uptake,

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.200
greater aerobic capacity
as measured on a progressive,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.140
graded, maximal treadmill test
to exhaustion,

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:32.520
though peak torque, peak strength
based on leg extensions

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.740
didn’t differ between diet groups.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:38.040
Bottom line: these data suggest
that vegetarian diets

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.660
do not compromise
performance outcomes

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.660
and may facilitate
aerobic capacity in athletes.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.520
In this 2020 study, all the plant-
based participants were eating vegan

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.960
for an average of four years.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.900
So, they were essentially comparing
those who ate meat for 21 years

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.060
versus those who ate
meat for 25 years.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.400
But after four years
eating plants,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.140
you might expect to see
some sort of difference.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.860
Yet, no signiﬁcant differences
were noted for upper

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.800
and lower body muscle strength,
like in the last new study.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.460
Both groups of athletes were comparable
for total body weight

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:12.480
and lean body mass, though age
was signiﬁcantly higher in vegans

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.740
compared with omnivores;

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.780
so, that put them
at a little disadvantage.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.820
Yet still, there it is again.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.300
Significantly better aerobic capacity.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.820
Then, they had them pedal
until exhaustion,

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.660
and the vegan group lasted
about 25 percent longer—

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.300
12 minutes as opposed to
9 minutes.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.540
Is that just because their
aerobic capacity is so high?

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.980
No, even after controlling
for VO2 max levels, there was still

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.620
a signiﬁcant endurance advantage
in the vegans.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.800
The researchers conclude
that in the very least,

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.380
a strictly plant-based diet
doesn’t seem to be detrimental

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:47.880
to endurance
and muscle strength,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.280
and endurance might
actually be better in vegans,

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.794
contrary to popular belief.

